        "chapterId": 248,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:07:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63867,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 248,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Uterine corpus or rarely cervix</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:07:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63868,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 248,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours typically occur in middle-aged women (mean age: 50&nbsp;years), who usually present with abnormal vaginal bleeding or pelvic pain, but uterine tumour resembling ovarian sex cord tumour can also be an incidental finding {28656712; 961630; 19542872; 25150955}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:07:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63869,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 248,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These are rare neoplasms (&lt;&nbsp;1% of uterine mesenchymal tumours).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:07:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63870,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 248,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:07:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63871,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 248,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours have been hypothesized to arise from pluripotent mesenchymal, endometrial stromal, or displaced ovarian sex cord cells {20070149}, but the pathogenesis is uncertain. Rearrangements involving either <em>ESR1</em> or <em>GREB1</em> have been reported, with a variety of partners, including <em>NCOA1</em>, <em>NCOA2</em>, <em>NCOA3</em>, <em>CTNNB1</em>, <em>NR4A3</em>, and <em>SS18</em> {30350331; 30273195; 31094921; 31464709}. The tumours do not harbour the molecular alterations seen in low-grade endometrial stromal sarcoma (<em>JAZF1</em>-<em>SUZ12</em>), adult granulosa cell tumour (<em>FOXL2</em> alterations), or Sertoli&ndash;Leydig cell tumour (<em>DICER1</em> alterations) {19542872; 26598979; 25581731}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:07:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63872,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 248,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours are typically intramural and/or submucosal, forming a polypoid mass with an average size of 6&nbsp;cm. They commonly display well-circumscribed margins and have a predominantly solid, variably soft to firm, and yellow or tan cut surface.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:07:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63873,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 248,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>These tumours are typically well circumscribed, but some have an irregular edge with entrapment of surrounding smooth muscle. They grow in sheets, cords, nests, insulae, trabeculae, or tubules, sometimes with a retiform appearance {19309399}. Cells have scant to abundant cytoplasm and may be pale (including foamy) or eosinophilic, sometimes resulting in a rhabdoid appearance. Nuclei are ovoid and show minimal cytological atypia, and mitotic activity is low, with the exception of malignant tumours, which may show prominent cytological atypia and brisk mitotic activity. Vascular invasion may be seen occasionally. Tumours have a polyphenotypic immunohistochemical profile, with variable positivity for sex cord markers (inhibin, calretinin, WT1, CD56, CD99, SF1, FOXL2, and melan-A), epithelial markers, ER, PR, smooth muscle markers (actin, desmin, and h-caldesmon), and CD10 {17182656; 21084963; 26598979; 16118629; 25581731; 27311867}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-07T15:24:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63874,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 248,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:07:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63875,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 248,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:07:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63876,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 248,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> sex cord patterns without a component of endometrial stromal tumour.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> immunoreactivity for sex cord&ndash;stromal markers.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:07:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63877,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 248,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>This entity is staged as uterine sarcoma using the FIGO, Union for International Cancer Control (UICC), and American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) systems.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:07:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63878,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 248,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "<p>Uterine tumours resembling ovarian sex cord tumours are benign in most cases but should be considered to have a low malignant potential because they may recur. However, no definitive prognostic morphological features have been identified {28656712; 25150955}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-07T15:25:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]